/** @mainpage

This suite exercises tsf-smt - deciding logical formulas with an SMT
solver from the Test Agent it runs on - against Z3 and cvc5:

- @ref smt-check - deciding a set of assertions: a satisfiable problem
  comes back @c sat with a model whose values satisfy it, an
  unsatisfiable one comes back @c unsat;
- @ref smt-prove - proving a conjecture by refutation: a valid
  entailment is @c unsat (proved), an invalid one is @c sat with a
  counter-model that breaks it;
- @ref smt-core - the unsat core of named assertions, and the
  documented difference between what Z3 and cvc5 name in it;
- @ref smt-errors - a malformed problem mapping to an error, and an
  undecidable/timed-out problem coming back @c unknown without failing.

There is no key and no network: the solver is a library linked into the
agent's RPC server and called in-process (Z3's C API, cvc5's C++ API),
so the problem never leaves the agent. Each group runs against both
engines and skips one the agent does not have. The point of the suite
is that tsf-smt builds the problem right, drives each engine over the
RPC, and reads the verdict, the model and the unsat core back as
structured data.

*/
